1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,604
How does Spring know what to inject?

2
00:00:01,684 --> 00:00:03,040
The order service constructor

3
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:04,900
requests a PaymentGateway.

4
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:06,839
Spring reads that dependency.

5
00:00:06,919 --> 00:00:09,400
The bean factory finds compatible types.

6
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:12,080
An unrelated logger won't match.

7
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:14,584
Matching beans must also be eligible.

8
00:00:14,664 --> 00:00:17,380
Here, one eligible gateway remains.

9
00:00:17,380 --> 00:00:18,540
Spring supplies its instance

10
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:19,921
to the constructor.

11
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:21,700
Without an eligible candidate,

12
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:23,660
this required constructor dependency

13
00:00:23,660 --> 00:00:25,743
stops context startup.

14
00:00:25,823 --> 00:00:27,720
Our tests verify both outcomes.

15
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:29,760
Qualifiers and primary settings

16
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:31,960
can also affect selection.

17
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:33,677
Match, filter, inject.

18
00:00:35,677 --> 00:00:36,768
Thanks for watching.

